  "body": "https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-CD-replace\n\nThe algorithm to replace text does not specify what to do when passed `null`.\n\nCurrently, Mozilla Firefox and Google Chrome treat `null` as if it were `\"null\"` (I guess by converting `null` to `String` internally when called);  and I am not sure whether to follow that behaviour in a DOM library I am writing.\n\nI did notice that the `data` parameter is specified as `DOMString`, not `DOMString?`, in `appendData`, `insertData`, etc. Should an error should be thrown if `data` cannot be converted to `DOMString`?\n## Example <sup><sub>(try in Firefox/Chrome)</sub></sup>\n\n``` js\nvar text = new Text();\ntext.appendData(null);\nconsole.log(text.data)  // => \"null\"\n```\n",
